<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Emirate of Bari</h1><p class="st-meta">847 CE – 871 CE (approx.) · capital Bari</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Short-lived Muslim emirate in Apulia, centred on the port of Bari, which raided southern Italy until Louis II took it in 871.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Arab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> soldiers, some of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> hired by the warring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> princes, seized <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-bari">Bari</a> around 847 under a leader named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalfun">Kalfun</a>. From this base on the Adriatic coast they extended control over much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mufarraj-ibn-sallam">Mufarraj ibn Sallam</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawdan">Sawdan</a>, the last emir, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-bari">Bari</a> sought recognition from the Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> and became an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> with a congregational mosque. It lived largely from raiding and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, attacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> and Byzantine territory and monasteries, though Sawdan also dealt diplomatically with Christian neighbours, and a Jewish chronicle recalls him as a figure of learning.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Carolingian emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-italy">Louis II</a> besieged <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-bari">Bari</a> for several years with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> allies and Byzantine naval help, taking the city in 871 and capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sawdan">Sawdan</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bari">Bari</a> in 876, and it became the capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/theme-of-longobardia">Theme of Longobardia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/emirate-of-bari.jpg" alt="Emirate of Bari" width="320" height="227"><figcaption>Image: from the Middle Ages, unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MadridSkylitzesFol97ra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>847 CE – 871 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bari</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kalfun, Mufarraj ibn Sallam, Sawdan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bari Emirate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=859&amp;focus=emirate-of-bari" aria-label="Emirate of Bari on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5092 710 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Emirate of Bari lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5412 966L6134 1224M5412 966L5139 672"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0M5139 672h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5412 966h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5412 966h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bari. <a href="/atlas?year=859&amp;focus=emirate-of-bari">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

